---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Types of Salary Negotiations | salarynegotiationcalc.com';
const description = 'Overview of salary negotiation scenarios: new job offers, annual reviews, promotions, and equity negotiations.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Types of Salary Negotiations</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>New job offer negotiation</h2><p>Highest leverage point in your career. You&rsquo;ve been selected; the employer wants you. Most people who negotiate receive a higher offer. Anchor high and negotiate total comp (base + bonus + equity + signing).</p>
  <h2>Annual review / merit increase</h2><p>Lower leverage than a new offer but still important. Prepare with a &ldquo;brag document&rdquo;: concrete achievements, metrics, and comparisons to market rate. Frame as business case, not personal need. Best time to negotiate annual reviews is 3–4 months before the review cycle.</p>
  <h2>Promotion negotiation</h2><p>Promotions often come with preset salary bands. Research the new level&rsquo;s market rate and negotiate for the midpoint to high-end of the band. Also negotiate title — titles affect future negotiations externally.</p>
  <h2>Equity and total compensation</h2><p>For startup and tech roles, equity (RSUs, options) can dwarf base salary. Evaluate equity on a risk-adjusted basis: public company RSUs are near-certain value; startup equity requires heavy discounting. Negotiate equity refresh grants, vesting acceleration on change of control, and clawback protection.</p>
  <h2>Counter-offer from current employer</h2><p>If leaving for a new job, your current employer may counter-offer. Research shows 70–80% of people who accept counter-offers leave within 6 months anyway. A counter-offer validates your market value but rarely addresses the underlying reason you were looking. Evaluate carefully.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-to-negotiate-salary">how to negotiate salary</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
